>> title: Mentsuyu
>> category: Sauces & Gravies
>> tags: japanese, dashi, soy, pantry, make ahead, no cook
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: めんつゆ, mentsuyu, mentsuyu, 麺つゆ, tsuyu, soba tsuyu, noodle dipping sauce, noodle soup base
>> pairs-with: dashi
>> servings: 8
>> time: 40 min
>> slack: forgiving — it keeps three weeks in a bottle in the fridge and only gets rounder; the one way to spoil it is to boil the dashi in at the end, which cooks the smell off and leaves it tasting of soy alone
>> step.1: One bottle that seasons half this shelf: dashi 4 : soy 1 : mirin 1, which is dipping strength. Simmer it down by a third for a concentrate and dilute it back with dashi, never with water.
>> step.2: boil the alcohol off, 1 min
>> step.3: dissolve, 1 min
>> step.4: bring to the edge of a simmer, then off
>> step.5: cool, then bottle

One bottle that seasons half this shelf: dashi 4 : soy 1 : mirin 1, which is dipping strength. Simmer it down by a third for a concentrate to keep, and dilute it back with dashi rather than with water.

Boil @mirin{1/2%cup}(120 mL) with @sake{2%Tbs}(30 mL) in a #small saucepan{} ~boil{1%min}, until the raw alcohol smell has gone and what is left smells sweet.

Stir @soy sauce{1/2%cup}(120 mL) and @sugar{1%tsp} into @&(~1)mirin{} and bring it back ~simmer{1%min}, until the sugar has gone.

Pour @dashi{2%cups}(475 mL) into @&(~1)seasoned soy{}, bring it to the very edge of a simmer ~warm{2%min} and take it straight off the heat.

Cool @&(~1)mentsuyu{} to room temperature ~cool{30%min}, then bottle it.
